{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 54, :comments ["<b>To the many you shall increase, etc.</b> They said in tractate Bava Batra (Bava Batra 117a) and this is their language: Rav Pappa said to Abbaye, according to the one who says [the division was] to those who left Egypt, that is what is written ״לָרַב תַּרְבֶּה״ etc., but according to the one who says [it was] to those who entered the land, what is ״לָרַב״ etc.? It is difficult — thus far. That is, if the division is for those counted in the plains of Moab, what place is there to say ״לָרַב״ etc.? Now, this difficulty does not arise from any inference, but we could say he did not focus on it, for the verse is explicitly written. Furthermore, these are not the words of an Amora to reject them, but the words of Rabbi Yonatan, who is a Tanna. And according to what has preceded us from the general principles of the Talmud, that whenever the Gemara says ״קַשְׁיָא״ it stands to be resolved, only that a resolution did not present itself to them." "<b>It would appear</b> according to what I explained concerning the statement ״בְּמִסְפַּר שֵׁמוֹת״ — which refers to the fifty-seven families — it is precisely inferred that it correctly said ״לָרַב״ etc., meaning that they divided [the land] into fifty-seven portions, and not portion equal to portion, but rather ״לָרַב תַּרְבֶּה נַחֲלָתוֹ״. The analogy — a family that has twenty thousand takes double that of a family that has ten thousand." "<b>Further,</b> it would appear according to the approach of Rashi, of blessed memory, that Rabbi Yonatan holds that they would take a division only for those who were counted in the plains of Moab. Accordingly, the verse comes [to address this case]: if one had eleven sons aged twenty, and another had eight sons — four aged twenty and four below twenty — and upon entry to the land, the one who had ten sons aged twenty lost four [who died], while the one who had four among his eight below twenty reached the age of twenty at the time of the division. Therefore the verse says ״לָרַב״ — meaning, at the time of the counting ״תַּרְבֶּה נַחֲלָתוֹ״ — meaning, the one who had ten sons aged twenty in this counting, you increase his portion proportional to ten, even though he has only six. And ״לַמְעַט״ — at the time of the counting, when he had only four aged twenty ״תַּמְעִיט״ — to give him proportional to four, even though he has eight aged twenty. And perhaps Rashi, of blessed memory, in his statement sought to resolve this difficulty with cleverness." "<b>Each man according to those counted of him, etc.</b> In the Sifrei (Sifrei Numbers 132) they said, and this is their language: ״לְפִי פְקֻדָיו״ teaches that the land was not divided except to each tribe according to what it is — thus far. There is no contradiction in this to what I explained on the verse ״בְּמִסְפַּר שֵׁמוֹת״ that it was divided among fifty-seven families. It is possible that it was divided among the tribes and among the families in this manner: they divided the land into twelve portions and the portions into fifty-seven portions; a tribe that has two families takes two portions, and one that has four takes four portions, and the portion would be according to the number — according to one opinion [the number] of those who left Egypt and according to one opinion [the number] of the plains of Moab. And if you say, since they divide among the families why did they need to divide among the tribes? The reason is so that each tribe would have its lot separately, which would not be the case if they divided among the families, for it would be possible to make a lot for two families of Shimon together with families of Zevulun and vice versa, and they would not be each tribe together." "<b>And in tractate</b> Bava Batra (Bava Batra 122a) they said, and this is their language: It was raised as a question for them: Was the land of Israel divided among the tribes or divided according to the skulls of individuals [i.e., per capita]? Come and hear: ״בֵּין רַב לִמְעָט״, and it is further taught in a baraita: In the future the land of Israel is destined to be divided among thirteen tribes, for at first it was divided among only twelve tribes — thus far. And it escaped the Gemara [that there is] the baraita in the Sifrei that they derived from ״אִישׁ לְפִי פְקוּדָיו״, and it stands to reason that the Tanna of the thirteen tribes also derives his teaching from ״אִישׁ לְפִי פְקוּדָיו״ like the Tanna of the Sifrei. And see the verse ״בֵּין רַב״ etc." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained according to the approach of Rabbi Yonatan, who holds that ״לָאֵלֶּה תֵּחָלֵק הָאָרֶץ״ refers to those who come into the land. And still I do not know whether this means specifically those who have reached the age of twenty, or to all who come into the land even to those who have not reached twenty. Scripture therefore says ״לְפִי פְקֻדָיו״ — from this you learn that it is specifically those of twenty years. And through this we justify the words of Rashi, who interpreted the verse ״לָאֵלֶּה״ but not for his stated reason."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:15:13.640753055Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}